// ASan battery fixture for increment-5 (split-frontier drop flags).
//
// `s` is allocated every iteration (`i.to_string() + "!"`, a
// `build_string_concat` call) and used on BOTH arms of the `if` (different
// message per parity), so its death frontier has two disjoint candidate
// free sites, one per arm.
//
// VERIFIED DEVIATION FROM THE DESIGN DOC'S ORIGINAL MOTIVATING FIXTURE:
// the design doc's first draft used a one-sided `if i % 2 == 0 { println!
// ("{}", s); }` (no `else`) modeled on `drops.rs`'s synthetic
// `declines_split_death_frontier` unit-test CFG (one TERMINAL predecessor,
// one CLEAN predecessor descending from a live block, merging at a shared
// join). Compiling that program and diffing flag-on against flag-off showed
// NO flag machinery: increment 4 (`multi_block_freeable`) already claims it,
// unguarded. Reason (confirmed by reading the generated C, not assumed):
// every `Call` terminator (here, `printf`) gets its OWN continuation block
// for "control after the call returns". That continuation block is reached
// from exactly one predecessor (the arm that made the call), so it
// satisfies increment 4's single-clean-death-frontier rule ALL BY ITSELF,
// entirely within the allocating arm's own dominance region -- the
// paired-with-a-clean-sibling join increment 4's `clean_chain_ok` walk is
// designed to reject never enters the picture, because the free never
// needs to look past that one-hop continuation block.
//
// The shape that genuinely defeats increment 4 in real compiled code is
// MULTIPLE independently-valid candidate sites for the SAME owner: when
// BOTH arms of the `if` use `s` (this fixture), EACH arm's `printf`
// continuation block independently satisfies increment 4's per-block site
// rule, so `multi_block_freeable`'s function-wide "exactly one qualifying
// block" requirement is violated by having TWO, and it declines the owner
// ENTIRELY (leak, safe) -- even though each site would have been sound in
// isolation. Increment 5 has no such uniqueness requirement (multiple sites
// on different paths are fine; the shared flag's clear makes a later site
// on the same path a no-op), so it is the only mechanism that reclaims
// this owner. `analysis::flags`'s own unit tests
// (`enrolls_split_frontier_and_places_join_site`,
// `uaf_shape_declines_live_join`, etc.) separately verify the
// clean-chain/dominance-declined shape the design doc's synthetic CFG
// describes, via directly constructed MIR; this real, compilable fixture
// verifies the multiplicity shape that real `if`/`else` lowering actually
// produces. Both are genuine declines of increments 1-4; this file
// exercises the one reachable from real BuildLang source today.
//
// See docs/MEMORY-PILLAR-DESIGN.md, "Increment 5: split-frontier drop
// flags" for the exact commands and results.
fn main() ~ Console {
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < 1000000 {
        let s = i.to_string() + "!";
        if i % 2 == 0 {
            println!("even {}", s);
        } else {
            println!("odd {}", s);
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
}
